  },
  {
    "cite": "Cal. Lab. Code §925",
    "library_file": "library/cal_lab_925.md",
    "proposition": "Employer may not require an employee who primarily resides and works in California, as a condition of employment, to accept an out-of-California forum or a choice of law depriving the employee of California's substantive protection for a California controversy; such a provision is voidable by the employee, and if voided the matter is adjudicated in California under California law; court may award the employee reasonable attorney's fees; §925(e) exception is limited to employees in fact individually represented by counsel in negotiating forum/choice-of-law terms; applies to contracts on or after January 1, 2017.",
