    "proposition": "Cited only to record that its 'narrow restraint' holding was disapproved by Edwards (2008); not available to sustain §8 as drawn.",
    "status_checked": "superseded — see Edwards (2008)"
  },
  {
    "cite": "FTC Non-Compete Clause Rule, 16 C.F.R. pt. 910, 89 Fed. Reg. 38342 (May 7, 2024)",
    "library_file": "library/ftc_noncompete_rule_16cfr910.md",
    "proposition": "Cited only to record that the Rule was set aside nationwide, never took effect, and cannot be relied on by either side; state law controls.",
    "status_checked": "vacated — see Ryan LLC v. FTC"
  },
  {
    "cite": "Ryan LLC v. Federal Trade Commission, 739 F. Supp. 3d 496 (N.D. Tex. 2024)",
    "library_file": "library/ryan_llc_v_ftc.md",
    "proposition": "FTC lacked substantive rulemaking authority under FTC Act §6(g) and the Rule was arbitrary and capricious; Rule set aside with nationwide APA effect; Fifth Circuit appeal voluntarily dismissed September 2025.",
    "status_checked": "in force"
  },
  {
    "cite": "Tex. Bus. & Com. Code §§15.50(a), 15.51(b)-(c), 15.52",
    "library_file": "library/tex_bcc_15_50.md",
    "proposition": "Covenant enforceable only if ancillary to or part of an otherwise enforceable agreement at the time made and reasonable as to time, geography and scope of activity, imposing no greater restraint than necessary to protect the promisee's goodwill or business interest; employer bears the burden when the agreement's primary purpose is personal services; unreasonable limitations must be reformed, and no damages may be awarded for breach before reformation; §§15.51 procedures and remedies are exclusive and preempt other enforceability criteria.",
    "status_checked": "in force"
  },
  {
    "cite": "Alex Sheshunoff Management Services, L.P. v. Johnson, 209 S.W.3d 644 (Tex. 2006)",
    "library_file": "library/sheshunoff_v_johnson.md",
    "proposition": "A covenant in an at-will employment agreement is enforceable where the employer's promise (e.g., to provide confidential information) is performed, even if not performed at the moment of contracting; modifies Light's timing requirement; the core inquiry is reasonableness under §15.50.",
    "status_checked": "in force"
  },
  {
    "cite": "Light v. Centel Cellular Co. of Texas, 883 S.W.2d 642 (Tex. 1994)",
    "library_file": "library/light_v_centel_1994.md",
    "proposition": "Cited only to record that its requirement that consideration be given at the time of making is no longer good law; it may not be cited for the proposition that a unilateral at-will agreement cannot support a covenant.",
    "status_checked": "modified — see Sheshunoff (2006)"
  },
  {
    "cite": "Marsh USA Inc. v. Cook, 354 S.W.3d 764 (Tex. 2011)",
    "library_file": "library/marsh_usa_v_cook.md",
    "proposition": "Consideration (stock options) reasonably related to the employer's interest in protecting goodwill can support a covenant not to compete; the Court rejected a narrow 'give rise' requirement — relevant to whether §8 is ancillary if Meridian in fact disclosed confidential information.",
    "status_checked": "in force"
  },
  {
    "cite": "DeSantis v. Wackenhut Corp., 793 S.W.2d 670 (Tex. 1990)",
    "library_file": "library/desantis_v_wackenhut.md",
    "proposition": "Applying Restatement §187, Texas refused to apply a Florida choice-of-law clause to a noncompete for an employee working in Texas because that state had a materially greater interest and a fundamental contrary policy; a choice-of-law clause does not control noncompete enforceability where the state with the materially greater interest has a fundamental contrary policy. Directly supports applying California law here, in Meridian's own chosen forum.",
    "status_checked": "in force"
  },
  {
    "cite": "Nedlloyd Lines B.V. v. Superior Court, 3 Cal.4th 459 (1992)",
    "library_file": "library/nedlloyd_v_superior_court.md",
    "proposition": "California follows Restatement §187: chosen law enforced if the chosen state has a substantial relationship, unless contrary to a fundamental California policy and California has a materially greater interest in the issue.",
    "status_checked": "in force"
  },
  {
    "cite": "Application Group, Inc. v. Hunter Group, Inc., 61 Cal.App.4th 881 (1998)",
    "library_file": "library/application_group_v_hunter.md",
    "proposition": "California had a materially greater interest in applying §16600 to a Maryland employer's noncompete with an employee recruited to work for a California employer; the out-of-state choice-of-law clause did not control; California's policy against noncompetes is 'fundamental.'",
    "status_checked": "in force"
  },
  {
    "cite": "Advanced Bionics Corp. v. Medtronic, Inc., 29 Cal.4th 697 (2002)",
    "library_file": "library/advanced_bionics_v_medtronic.md",
    "proposition": "California courts should generally not enjoin a party from pursuing litigation in another state's courts (TRO reversed in parallel noncompete litigation); cautions about interstate forum conflicts; does not retreat from §16600 policy. So Meridian can likely file in Texas — but that does not make the covenant enforceable.",
    "status_checked": "in force"
  },
  {
    "cite": "Ixchel Pharma, LLC v. Biogen, Inc., 9 Cal.5th 1130 (2020)",
    "library_file": "library/ixchel_v_biogen.md",
    "proposition": "Rule of reason applies to §16600 challenges to restraints in business-to-business contracts; the employment context is governed by Edwards, where noncompetes are void. Forecloses a reasonableness defense to §8 in California.",
    "status_checked": "in force"
  },
  {
    "cite": "Restatement (Second) of Conflict of Laws §187(2)",
    "library_file": "library/restatement_187.md",
    "proposition": "Chosen law applies unless the chosen state has no substantial relationship/reasonable basis, or application would be contrary to a fundamental policy of a state with a materially greater interest that would be the applicable-law state under §188 absent the choice. (§188's text is not in the library.)",
    "status_checked": "in force"
  },
  {
    "cite": "Tex. Civ. Prac. & Rem. Code §17.042; Michiana Easy Livin' Country v. Holten, 168 S.W.3d 777 (Tex. 2005) (library practice note)",
    "library_file": "library/tx_jurisdiction_note.md",
    "proposition": "A Travis County forum clause generally supplies consent to personal jurisdiction, but if the clause is voided under Cal. Lab. Code §925 at the employee's election the Texas court must find independent minimum contacts, and two short Austin trips plus a contract with a Texas employer 'may or may not' suffice; a Texas injunction would face non-recognition in California on public-policy grounds.",
    "status_checked": "in force"
  }
]

